    <node id="Chaacmol">
      <data key="d0">Chaacmol</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Chaacmol refers to a significant historical and cultural figure within Maya civilization, particularly associated with Yucatan. He is notably linked to a funeral chamber that houses artistic representations, illustrating the funerary practices and beliefs of ancient cultures. The chamber features murals that depict Chaacmol as a complex character, symbolically associated with the god of ballplayers and representing themes of war and reverence.

In addition to his martial associations, Chaacmol is believed to symbolize various attributes, including the notion of a "spotted tiger" or "leopard" in the Maya language. This connection reflects his cultural significance and potentially his status as a warrior. He is described as a chieftain whose disturbed remains contributed to a substantial archaeological discovery, shedding light on ancient Maya burial customs and practices. The reverence afforded to Chaacmol's statue and its removal is treated with profound respect in Maya tradition, marking him as an important ancestor or figure within their lineage.

Chaacmol's narrative extends into mythology, where he is depicted as a character who met his demise due to jealousy at the hands of his brother, Aac. This familial relationship is crucial to understanding Chaacmol's place within the broader context of Maya beliefs, including themes of betrayal and conflict with siblings such as Moo. His representation in murals, archaeological findings, and his statue at Chichen Itza further affirm his standing as a prominent figure in Maya art, culture, and ritual practices.

In summary, Chaacmol encapsulates a unique blend of historical, artistic, and mythological significance in Maya civilization, making him an essential figure for understanding the cultural heritage and spiritual life of the Maya. His associations with warfare, burial customs, and familial ties highlight the intricate nature of his legacy within ancient Mesoamerican society.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa1a6047d3b960e8aa1f1e7a19add07e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965b5b39cb58a29f56a43495ef18b6ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-964858730c8a8dc2179e59d3cb07c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f65c2b617b2b75df32a484e145b034fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f47d3b712e0953b518a70453701f3ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90130c63183d6c112f655e298f1be97a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5b2f7d138338b628491cb8bacb7fe79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cf3140092a745267effea6e9a53aa65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d60c6202de29d519891205dac45f966a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08ffcfc6ec928be11797840c2e48ac2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b549627ac943f03650e7d7278845faf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0986a653a68c393fd5247a90b35adc69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a944ace9f08bbbd03f30787f1ce4bc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-469abfadf4d8fb7475eafd715164f218&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f83c27282913cff5172f6cf0dfa303eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06e492003a084ec018b849a310a4e995&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b991502e22e90fb761c5591cdca0b88e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eea61287f7c7aac52c315ee2cca003fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d7d407dd88d4f39cbd7f09e60b40325</data>
