    <node id="Father">
      <data key="d0">Father</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Father is an important and multifaceted figure across various narratives, representing different roles and relationships. In Elsie's life, he serves as a significant source of joy and strength, accompanying her during childhood adventures and enhancing her experiences. In another context, the father of a speaker emerges as a country parson who interacts with various social classes and exhibits a deep knowledge of human experience, suggesting a figure of broad understanding and connectivity.

The clergyman known as Father is highly regarded by his parishioners, exemplifying a compassionate nature and establishing strong ties across social strata. This character's role extends to imparting teachings about governmental authority and social dynamics, indicating a figure of moral and social guidance within the community.

In familial contexts, Father takes on a more personal dimension, as seen in a letter penned by a father expressing deep feelings and regrets towards his son, Cecil, reflecting on their complex relationship. Similarly, Harringford's father is noted for efforts aimed at reconciliation through a remorseful letter, illustrating the challenges within paternal relationships. Cissy's father, though physically absent, becomes a crucial figure that she seeks to connect with, emphasizing the emotional void created by such absence.

Additionally, the narrator describes his father as a presence during visits to locations like the Bear tavern and mentions his return from Brampton, highlighting the father's involvement in the family’s daily life and business discussions. Together, these descriptions weave a rich tapestry of paternal figures who navigate various roles as guides, companions, and complex human beings within their respective stories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6a025623f47200c8c812a059793950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abefe66985061e17bec49f1898419196&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-184fcee92f8ae5f8c3132b64ac949ef4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87a982ac1d3e226f09d7de61a10ed706&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f6861a405786e2c037bf50463f3c97b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d6853b4fb9963abd3558cab9d234fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8125a827c1ae26a6b606adacd1ab3c01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-291c8a0f74d4df3d7cf1b446e90edd31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b8a70ed68b8ad9957789f681884b2ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80d293af5e7df136bc3f73cb98ae3fad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ed7144a6153903011bff75ac1d5f586</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763627106</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sona">
      <data key="d0">Sona</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sona, also referred to as Gold, was an Indian nurse who served the Inglis family for over thirty years, showcasing loyalty and care.&lt;SEP&gt;Sona is a caretaker who looks after Elsie during the palkie journey, showing the dynamics of the family's travels.&lt;SEP&gt;Sona was a longstanding member of the Indian Christian Church in Lucknow and helped during the journey of the Inglis family back to England.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6a025623f47200c8c812a059793950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e67cfa2aae65293abe96f3dae9b1d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9d85bd579f0f4db276bf050b7458944</data>
