    <node id="Dr. Inglis">
      <data key="d0">Dr. Inglis</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dr. Inglis is a significant figure in the history of women's rights and medical advancement, closely associated with her daughter, Elsie Inglis, and mentioned prominently in the context of advocating for women's involvement in medical services. As a physician, Dr. Inglis supported her daughter's pioneering work and was known for her strong commitment to women's rights, actively participating in discussions concerning the impact of marital consent on medical care.

As a leader and organizer, Dr. Inglis held the position of Honorary Secretary of the Scottish Federation of Suffrage Societies, showcasing her dedication to women's suffrage and her considerable organizational skills. Her advocacy extended to public appearances, where she urged women to take on roles such as sanitary inspectors and actively shared her experiences regarding women's suffrage. She was also noted for her inspirational speaking engagements, where she recounted tales and conveyed moral lessons that left a lasting impression on her audience.

Dr. Inglis played a crucial role in maternal and infant welfare, as well as the management of the Scottish Women’s Hospitals, which she helped found. During wartime, she advocated for the participation of women in medical services and spearheaded medical missions, particularly during crises such as the typhus epidemic among Serbian soldiers. She demonstrated remarkable resilience and courage in her efforts, managing communications and logistical arrangements for her teams while providing exemplary care despite her declining health and the challenges of war.

Her contributions were not only professional but also personal, as she showed a nurturing attitude towards her patients and took a keen interest in the medical education of her nieces. Dr. Inglis’s legacy is marked by her significant efforts in healthcare and women's rights, earning her great respect and recognition in her burial, underscoring the impact she had on society during her time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a32f709c5563ea114d4fd6bb560a0d87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b3715e8f3a5074ee835c66b56835395&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05ce4cb100ab0508dd550c088c47bb07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55c89e82ea0a483e5673a0449dbed190&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfdfb54a79e5b06ab1a40209cf48d325&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fc5bb98f8d283a62db5e74bbfa9071&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-510929c91e3f9a3e013feb10138683ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f3cb4d420a1cee0ebcac4aa18485c89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88f6b3f825b0114b7c33343abfedda35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69f2e8f5cc7c639af947fe43f6cdc962&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb2443dd1abc31bd06002ccb7a44c963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5ab1577aaf007918da08d1e5f32fbf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-563025dcd3d25d4d2626b41bcc0a84e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6540245da04893916f916cbd8b74aa3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7f3fc74bc960c00595a6361c3449b8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7071df28afb23eb2c415df5bf3aa596&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64ab42f17996dafda93ff20305420aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0440f793586398094204255524bc8f27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbc46a780201b0aee22ebb94b7217e12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4be564c5fa39fff8a20f1369b2fa02d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64db1dd7d0712bd0b11ae20de546f3e8</data>
