    <node id="Mars">
      <data key="d0">Mars</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mars symbolizes struggle and opportunity, referenced as influencing Elsie Inglis’s relentless fight for equality.&lt;SEP&gt;Mars is referred to as a god associated with war, impacting the lives and relationships of characters in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Mars is the Roman god of war, associated here with the themes of conflict and romantic pursuits.&lt;SEP&gt;Mars is presented as a step-father figure to Cupid, representing the theme of warfare intertwined with love.&lt;SEP&gt;Mars is the god of war, acknowledged within the text for his martial context and significance to the soldiers.&lt;SEP&gt;Mars is referenced as the Roman god of war and considered Cupid's step-father due to his relationship with Venus.&lt;SEP&gt;Mars is referenced as a figure associated with astrology and the adverse and favorable aspects of the stars.</data>
