    </node>
    <node id="Fils de la Liberte">
      <data key="d0">Fils de la Liberte</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The _Fils de la Liberte_ is a French-Canadian group that opposed the Doric Club and was engaged in acts of rebellion during the unrest.&lt;SEP&gt;Fils de la Liberte was an association formed in Montreal advocating for liberty, mirroring American revolutionary sentiments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86a0a44acd086e0907bf225584c3be7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dac7fb6c5e874c886dbb8df30d7d6fd5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622249</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Montreal">
      <data key="d0">Montreal</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Montreal is a city in Lower Canada where significant political events, including a riot, occurred in 1832.&lt;SEP&gt;Montreal is a city in Canada that served as a center for both Patriote and Loyalist meetings during the political unrest.&lt;SEP&gt;Montreal is the city where significant rioting occurred between opposing groups during the turmoil of 1837.&lt;SEP&gt;Montreal is the city to which Debartzch fled during the conflict.&lt;SEP&gt;Montreal is the city where Wolfred Nelson was taken after his capture.&lt;SEP&gt;Montreal is an island city mentioned as the target for a rebel advance during the conflict described.&lt;SEP&gt;Montreal is referenced for rioting, showcasing local unrest during the historical context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86a0a44acd086e0907bf225584c3be7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe367bc72a82be3c2cacfc81ba72e719&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6e90edbdd9d437566164aceb39d9b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6059f7b6685e9fbba6db55d93c65ee</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622249</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="November 6">
      <data key="d0">November 6</data>
      <data key="d1">date</data>
      <data key="d2">November 6 marks the date when rioting broke out in Montreal, highlighting the tensions between different factions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86a0a44acd086e0907bf225584c3be7a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622251</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thomas Storrow Brown">
      <data key="d0">Thomas Storrow Brown</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Thomas Storrow Brown is a 'general' of the rebellion and a supporter of Papineau.&lt;SEP&gt;Thomas Storrow Brown was one of the lieutenants of L. J. Papineau who communicated with William Lyon Mackenzie regarding the timing of rebellions.&lt;SEP&gt;Thomas Storrow Brown is another chief lieutenant of Papineau, who took command of armed habitants during the resistance.&lt;SEP&gt;Thomas Storrow Brown was appointed commander by the rebels but had no military experience.&lt;SEP&gt;Thomas Storrow Brown is remembered by Canadian numismatists for issuing a halfpenny token and returned to his hardware business post-rebellion.&lt;SEP&gt;Thomas Storrow Brown commands the Patriotes at St Charles during the struggle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86a0a44acd086e0907bf225584c3be7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6059f7b6685e9fbba6db55d93c65ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f60868fc120dda4e8b845c8a6db7921&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b195a27b43ec85da2d0175fa01cef0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622251</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="William Lyon Mackenzie">
      <data key="d0">William Lyon Mackenzie</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">William Lyon Mackenzie was the leader of the rebellion in Upper Canada and is mentioned in correspondence with the _Patriotes_ in Lower Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86a0a44acd086e0907bf225584c3be7a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622251</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="St Eustache">
      <data key="d0">St Eustache</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">St Eustache is a village in Canada noted for being the site of historical skirmishes involving rebels.&lt;SEP&gt;St Eustache is a town near Cote St Joseph where Scottish settlers fled due to the unrest in Lower Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;St Eustache is a village where news of the insurgents' defeat reached and where the Abbe Paquin preached moderation.&lt;SEP&gt;St Eustache is the village where significant tumult and gathering of the insurgents occurred during the rebellion.&lt;SEP&gt;St Eustache is a village where troops camped after the engagement and which suffered damage during the conflict.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86a0a44acd086e0907bf225584c3be7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-349529243dbc83f8fcf9f0443bec18f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-353f63fa013a00ee4a62ffebc8c022a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5baf8115ec01000bf9bea26709c3f099&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6e90edbdd9d437566164aceb39d9b52</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622251</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Yamaska">
      <data key="d0">Yamaska</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Yamaska is a location in Lower Canada associated with the issuance of bank notes featuring a specific emblem linked to the _Patriotes_.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86a0a44acd086e0907bf225584c3be7a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622251</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sir James Craig">
      <data key="d0">Sir James Craig</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sir James Craig was the governor of Canada starting in 1807, known for his military background and controversial governance.&lt;SEP&gt;Sir James Craig is a historical figure depicted in the chronicle and represented in a portrait held at the Dominion Archives.&lt;SEP&gt;Sir James Craig was the governor of Canada who dismissed military officers connected to a newspaper and suppressed _Le Canadien_.&lt;SEP&gt;Sir James Craig was a governor who resisted the Assembly's resolution to exert financial control over the British Parliament.&lt;SEP&gt;Sir James Craig is characterized by his 'Reign of Terror' during significant political conflicts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e6edfc8c6c5b4bb45e9a1b7ed41347d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba0f247b33f556371c343cbd158638c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b195a27b43ec85da2d0175fa01cef0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622251</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colonel Panet">
      <data key="d0">Colonel Panet</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Colonel Panet was an officer who fought in the defense of Quebec and served as the speaker of the House of Assembly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622251</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pierre Bedard">
      <data key="d0">Pierre Bedard</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Pierre Bedard was a prominent member of the House of Assembly and a notable figure on the staff of _Le Canadien_.&lt;SEP&gt;Pierre Bedard is a French-Canadian who made notable comments regarding loyalty and language in the Assembly debates.&lt;SEP&gt;Pierre Bedard was an officer dismissed by Sir James Craig and later arrested in connection with the suppression of _Le Canadien_.&lt;SEP&gt;Pierre Bedard is associated with French-Canadian nationalism and has notable events related to his arrest and release.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41f4557b5a58dc496c18ce0c8f8f2c88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b195a27b43ec85da2d0175fa01cef0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622251</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Le Canadien">
      <data key="d0">Le Canadien</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Le Canadien was a newspaper established by French Canadians to represent their views and defend against criticisms from English-led publications.&lt;SEP&gt;Le Canadien was a newspaper suppressed by Sir James Craig, known for its seditious and defamatory content.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622252</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="House of Assembly">
      <data key="d0">House of Assembly</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The House of Assembly is a legislative body in Lower Canada where important political events took place during Craig's administration.&lt;SEP&gt;The House of Assembly is the elected lower house of the legislature in Lower Canada that faced constraints in governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba0f247b33f556371c343cbd158638c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622252</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sir George Prevost">
      <data key="d0">Sir George Prevost</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sir George Prevost was the successor to Sir James Craig who reversed the previous policies and worked to conciliate French Canadians.&lt;SEP&gt;Sir George Prevost is mentioned in relation to the French Canadians and their political context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-392528a8e340519b1401fbf1b71aad8c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622252</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Louis Joseph Papineau">
      <data key="d0">Louis Joseph Papineau</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Louis Joseph Papineau was a young political leader who took his seat in the House of Assembly and became a prominent figure in Lower Canadian history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622252</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="War of 1812">
      <data key="d0">War of 1812</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The War of 1812 was a conflict that influenced political dynamics, resulting in improved relations between French Canadians and the British administration.&lt;SEP&gt;The War of 1812 is mentioned in relation to the loyalty of French-Canadians.&lt;SEP&gt;The War of 1812 is mentioned in relation to older men in the village who served in it, signifying its historical importance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-392528a8e340519b1401fbf1b71aad8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42797507f8eb83c3fc15fa42c44189b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622788</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bureaucrats">
      <data key="d0">Bureaucrats</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Bureaucrats were a group surrounding Sir James Craig, some of whom allegedly wished to precipitate disturbance in Lower Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bureaucrats, also known as the Chateau Clique, is a term for the local oligarchy in Lower Canada that maintained control of the government.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bureaucrats refer to the public officials in the colony who were in a position of power during the governance disputes.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bureaucrats refers to the administrative class during the 1837 Rebellion, often contrasted with the Patriotes in their political stance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6059f7b6685e9fbba6db55d93c65ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba0f247b33f556371c343cbd158638c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622252</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mathematical Problems">
      <data key="d0">Mathematical Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mathematical problems were the tasks Pierre Bedard occupied himself with during his imprisonment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622252</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gosford">
      <data key="d0">Gosford</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Gosford is the governor who recognized the seriousness of the unrest caused by the Patriote movement.&lt;SEP&gt;Gosford is mentioned as a figure who is called to gather the legislature of Lower Canada during the outbreak of rebellion.&lt;SEP&gt;Gosford is described as the governor of Canada, involved in political matters of the time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d3c806038738c5cf824708ed3cfff5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe367bc72a82be3c2cacfc81ba72e719&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f63400d8188502b67eda00c561659eb8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622252</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colborne">
      <data key="d0">Colborne</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Colborne is mentioned as a decision-maker regarding the fate of the prisoners in the context of the rebellion in Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d3c806038738c5cf824708ed3cfff5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74232d6a8ea1ca9bbf02c4e6c8ef3d90</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
