    </node>
    <node id="Charles Buller">
      <data key="d0">Charles Buller</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Charles Buller is identified as Durham's chief secretary and a friend of Thomas Carlyle, involved in the deliberations regarding the rebels.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles Buller acts as secretary to Durham and has a role in the political conflicts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d3c806038738c5cf824708ed3cfff5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b195a27b43ec85da2d0175fa01cef0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622253</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thomas Carlyle">
      <data key="d0">Thomas Carlyle</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Thomas Carlyle is referenced as a notable figure and the mentor of Charles Buller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d3c806038738c5cf824708ed3cfff5e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622253</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Robert Bouchette">
      <data key="d0">Robert Bouchette</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Robert Bouchette was another significant political prisoner during the rebellions in Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;Robert Bouchette is mentioned alongside Wolfred Nelson as a rebel leader who faced consequences for the uprising.&lt;SEP&gt;Robert Bouchette is a rebel leader during the 1837 Rebellion known for advocating for the rights of the people.&lt;SEP&gt;Robert Bouchette is a historical figure who recovered from a serious wound and later became the commissioner of customs in Ottawa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d3c806038738c5cf824708ed3cfff5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb71f32b0ab08e17e4d6cb83be9c380c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58ea9058e9ed8c78aad18db9d953d662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6059f7b6685e9fbba6db55d93c65ee</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622253</data>
