    </node>
    <node id="Ninety-Two Resolutions">
      <data key="d0">Ninety-Two Resolutions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ninety-Two Resolutions were a political manifesto expressing grievances and demands for change in governance in Lower Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ninety-Two Resolutions were a series of complaints presented by the Canadian Assembly to the British government.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ninety-Two Resolutions are a political platform introduced by Elzear Bedard and inspired by Papineau, calling attention to abuses in governance.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ninety-Two Resolutions were a set of demands regarding self-governance in Canada, aimed at addressing political grievances against British colonial rule.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ninety-Two Resolutions is a set of demands presented by the Patriotes for political reforms in Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ninety-Two Resolutions are a set of demands made by the Patriotes, representing their political aspirations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ae911c17e49d755ada12627d0a57c5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-392528a8e340519b1401fbf1b71aad8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b195a27b43ec85da2d0175fa01cef0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622255</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lord Aylmer">
      <data key="d0">Lord Aylmer</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Lord Aylmer is the governor of Canada appointed in 1830, tasked with negotiating revenue control with the Assembly.&lt;SEP&gt;Lord Aylmer was the governor of Canada recalled due to difficulties with the Assembly.&lt;SEP&gt;Lord Aylmer was the governor mentioned in relation to the Ninety-Two Resolutions and political grievances in the Canadas.&lt;SEP&gt;Lord Aylmer is the governor of Canada, who is mentioned in the context of political conflicts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ae911c17e49d755ada12627d0a57c5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da749e17a56c35554061ac541d67d08c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b195a27b43ec85da2d0175fa01cef0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622255</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="John Neilson">
      <data key="d0">John Neilson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">John Neilson is a Scottish editor of the Quebec _Gazette_ who was appointed to oppose a bill in London and successfully influenced its shelving.&lt;SEP&gt;John Neilson was a Scottish editor of the Quebec Gazette who opposed the Union Bill and worked to protect the interests of French Canadians.&lt;SEP&gt;John Neilson is a politician who initially supported Papineau but later distanced himself due to disagreements over political resolutions.&lt;SEP&gt;John Neilson is noted as a minority voter against Papineau's party during the resolution voting process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba0f247b33f556371c343cbd158638c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da749e17a56c35554061ac541d67d08c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622256</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Augustin Cuvillier">
      <data key="d0">Augustin Cuvillier</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Augustin Cuvillier is a politician and friend of John Neilson who followed Neilson's lead against Papineau's proposals.&lt;SEP&gt;Augustin Cuvillier is another individual who opposed the Ninety-Two Resolutions and aligned with the minority voting block.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622256</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="F. A. Quesnel">
      <data key="d0">F. A. Quesnel</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">F. A. Quesnel is a moderate member of the Patriote party who attempted to secure judicial independence but faced Papineau's wrath.&lt;SEP&gt;F. A. Quesnel is mentioned as one of the individuals who voted against Papineau's Ninety-Two Resolutions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622256</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Andrew Stuart">
      <data key="d0">Andrew Stuart</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Andrew Stuart is a respected lawyer and politician who aligned with Neilson and Cuvillier in opposing Papineau.&lt;SEP&gt;Andrew Stuart is identified as a dissenting member from Papineau's party regarding the Ninety-Two Resolutions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622256</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Quebec">
      <data key="d0">Quebec</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Quebec is a province in Canada where Scottish names and the historical presence of French Canadians are noted.&lt;SEP&gt;Quebec is a province in Canada that is significant in the context of the political events surrounding the Ninety-Two Resolutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Quebec is the Canadian province where the Earl of Durham arrived to oversee the political situation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb71f32b0ab08e17e4d6cb83be9c380c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-353f63fa013a00ee4a62ffebc8c022a9</data>
