    <node id="Elzear Bedard">
      <data key="d0">Elzear Bedard</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Elzear Bedard is mentioned as an individual who moved the Ninety-Two Resolutions and later broke with Papineau.&lt;SEP&gt;Elzear Bedard is a politician who introduces the Ninety-Two Resolutions and is suspended as a judge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b195a27b43ec85da2d0175fa01cef0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622256</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Etienne Parent">
      <data key="d0">Etienne Parent</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Etienne Parent is identified as a prominent figure in French-Canadian literature and a seceder from Papineau's support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622256</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="St Denis">
      <data key="d0">St Denis</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">St Denis is a village in Canada known for a key battle involving French-Canadian rebels against British forces.&lt;SEP&gt;St Denis is a village where armed habitants gathered to resist against British authority.&lt;SEP&gt;St Denis is a village where a significant engagement occurred during the rebellion, witnessing troop actions and civilian involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;St Denis is the location of a check administered to Colonel Gore's column during the rebellion.&lt;SEP&gt;St Denis is a location where Brown retreated and where significant engagement occurred during the conflict.&lt;SEP&gt;St Denis is a location where significant conflicts occurred during the rebellion involving the Patriotes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0099aaf0d6a5d5b602247be491e3fb1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f60868fc120dda4e8b845c8a6db7921&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-353f63fa013a00ee4a62ffebc8c022a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-392528a8e340519b1401fbf1b71aad8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622257</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colonel Gore">
      <data key="d0">Colonel Gore</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Colonel Gore was a British military commander involved in the engagement at St Denis, leading troops during the conflict.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonel Gore is involved in the first engagement of the rebellion, marked by a check at St Denis.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonel Gore is the British officer who returned to St Denis to oversee the aftermath of the conflict and received criticism for not preventing excesses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0099aaf0d6a5d5b602247be491e3fb1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622257</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lieutenant Weir">
      <data key="d0">Lieutenant Weir</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Lieutenant Weir was a British subaltern killed by the Patriotes during the engagement, whose murder incited further violence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0099aaf0d6a5d5b602247be491e3fb1e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763622257</data>
