    <node id="Dr. Earle">
      <data key="d0">Dr. Earle</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dr. Earle is presented as a tall man with a deep voice who engages with Rudolph, known for his musical tone and friendly demeanor.&lt;SEP&gt;Dr. Earle is a character who exhibits irritation when discussing Fang and reflects on the situation with a sense of foreboding.&lt;SEP&gt;Dr. Earle is the teacher of Rudolph's teacher, who is an aged Chinaman.&lt;SEP&gt;Dr. Earle is a character who expresses frustration towards accusations made about stealing children and seems weary from the situation.&lt;SEP&gt;Dr. Earle is a character with deep-set eyes who speaks about the burden of his work and expresses concern for a beaten man.&lt;SEP&gt;Dr. Earle is a character who examines the painted scroll and tries to interpret its meaning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f11361a750eadd8a4749bae2438fb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb115037ebf479e3fa9826264e2a73f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-941c4d13af541f7963a27e1494bf443b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d338a477bdce675ba495c45901f2f4f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95a4351613d57b076f8450fdf6a8558e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfe3a458e44f6e2041ed190afdf2aa9f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763623662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mrs. Forrester">
      <data key="d0">Mrs. Forrester</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mrs. Forrester is a multifaceted character who navigates various emotional states and interactions throughout the narrative. She engages deeply with Rudolph, provoking thoughtful reflections on life and the world, showcasing her intellectual curiosity and desire for connection. Despite this, Mrs. Forrester is also depicted as trembling and miserable at times, seeking assistance from others, which reveals her vulnerability and distressing circumstances.

In contrast to her moments of despair, Mrs. Forrester exhibits liveliness during gatherings, entertaining her companions with engaging narratives and performances. This duality in her character highlights her ability to captivate an audience even while grappling with personal troubles. Furthermore, she demonstrates a mocking attitude toward Rudolph upon his return, displaying curiosity about what he has brought back. This interaction suggests a level of familiarity, albeit mixed with a sense of criticism or disappointment.

Rudolph observes that Mrs. Forrester did not wait for him, hinting at a potential sense of loss or missed connection between them. This nuance adds depth to her character, suggesting that her relationship with Rudolph is complex and fraught with emotional undercurrents. Additionally, she is mentioned as waiting for her husband, indicating domestic concerns that run parallel to the unfolding drama in their lives.

Mrs. Forrester briefly expresses frustration with the cicadas' noise, further illustrating moments of irritation amidst her more dynamic interactions. During a dinner scene, she also makes an angry gesture, suggesting underlying tension among the group, revealing her ties to the interpersonal dynamics surrounding her.

Overall, Mrs. Forrester emerges as a character of mystery and charm, embodying a blend of intellect, vulnerability, liveliness, and frustration, which enriches her presence in the narrative and her relationship with Rudolph and others.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68888bb43a75a4c144e123dadcb3dd1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df25ca25a175761aaaa6532ccedf415&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb115037ebf479e3fa9826264e2a73f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17752cd66181b87dbe6d4ea9fb2bff3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cddefae4082fc01755e5d4102df7a0fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2e10ee922efbc450e8141eaec0afb8b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0830112ae5a46289fc2882b6e60a848&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efb4b986690c80d677428e9a12caf306&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f133b603db1fb8ead3e54d2e177d879</data>
