    <node id="The Dean">
      <data key="d0">The Dean</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Dean is a complex character depicted as a portly man with a dignified demeanor, serving as the father of Salome and Sheba. He is deeply concerned about his health and faces various personal and familial challenges, particularly in managing the family's finances and introducing the concept of retrenchment to his daughters. His authoritative nature is reflected in his dialogue, where he expresses anxieties about social propriety and is critical of others, including Mr. Darbey.

In the narrative, The Dean plays an important role in the social dynamics, being actively involved in arranging dinner invitations and managing interactions among guests. He has a complicated familial relationship with Georgiana Tidman, expressing care for her well-being as well as anxiety concerning the family's financial situation, particularly regarding the restoration of the spire and other expenses tied to their lifestyle.

The Dean's interactions also encompass conversations with other key characters, such as Sir Tristram and Noah Topping, where they discuss various matters including health and controversies surrounding animal care. At one point, he impulsively provides money to his servant Blore, indicating his emotional state and the burden of his responsibilities. Despite these pressures, The Dean is portrayed as a caring and responsible figure, striving to maintain dignity amid adversity.

His character is also marked by moments of distress, reflecting on accusations of wrongdoing concerning his treatment of an animal and expressing regret about his moral standing in complicated situations. This adds depth to his portrayal as he confronts internal and external conflicts while trying to navigate the challenges of his role as a father and a community figure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-406730a86493e16f65817908a0bead18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f4fbe7f763ab4a0c366e0536ed6f49e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de37e70a12227d78dd4e8b830ac40b80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bf94783f183c51cfeb7911ef8186ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a623d5c8d4e641231b09b42787ad809b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50b102270ef0e98eb298e79412603ac2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1005fe668029a3498fae762b9e2347d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea62626f9134cd6522f82d474b2c357b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d690635b31230e7613331edb68d7205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3c3f1e54f2cd94a7ba1914df2ac61e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59edcbf702bf09e6c7842114ee9fee90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33c9d5647c1407e2585c351081e7eb47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3037df50481adaea6024d7ae1774bd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af1eb7dd19180841d20fad29810d6539&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5935cd19a49d869dfe9b8b95e3558c7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76f6232d66db0520c4e8b57318171b32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fe1560cf60e9d4a7338b3d121ff45e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee051bd08ba32e2c7f5e0938dbbc6c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-893320667c086c5f7bc12fc4874ac8b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95bebed720934a7d07e65bb3901bf810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4d7bb5676e6bc4c87b3981f6f0ae16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be2c82e11dcce129d5615a4deb4b0724&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fdfaa450fecffba6a5f866eaa6aeec0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-852d4b6446a1278be5194702894654c2</data>
