    </node>
    <node id="St. Marvells">
      <data key="d0">St. Marvells</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">St. Marvells is a town that serves as a central setting for various activities and relationships within the narrative. It is primarily noted for being a location where races occur, highlighting a dynamic aspect of town life. Additionally, St. Marvells is significant due to the connections characters such as Blore and Georgiana have with this locale, showcasing its role as a community hub.

Within St. Marvells, Mr. Topping has been appointed as the Head Constable, underscoring the town's governance structure and the responsibilities associated with law enforcement. This role is echoed in the mention of Noah Topping's duties, indicating that notable events and civic duties are integral to the town's narrative.

Furthermore, the town is where characters Hannah and Noah Topping have recently moved and established new friendships, suggesting a sense of community and social integration. The reference to The Dean having previously held a position in St. Marvells also contributes to the narrative by highlighting the reputations and histories of individuals associated with this setting.

St. Marvells is closely linked with the police station, tying it further to community affairs and public safety. This connection reinforces its portrayal as a center where various character-driven plots unfold, including the potential for rumors regarding the actions of its inhabitants. Notably, St. Marvells is addressed in the Introduction Note of the play, where a spelling inconsistency is clarified, indicating attention to detail in its presentation within the narrative context. 

Overall, St. Marvells is depicted as a multifaceted town rich with social interactions, civic duties, and a backdrop for unfolding events that shape the lives of its characters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-406730a86493e16f65817908a0bead18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f4fbe7f763ab4a0c366e0536ed6f49e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de37e70a12227d78dd4e8b830ac40b80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9adb1b7231075c5bd3151775f9ce69c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1005fe668029a3498fae762b9e2347d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5935cd19a49d869dfe9b8b95e3558c7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76f6232d66db0520c4e8b57318171b32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fdfaa450fecffba6a5f866eaa6aeec0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b99d32a6c4077b81dac5436dc1d07de</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1763626854</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dandy Dick">
      <data key="d0">Dandy Dick</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">Dandy Dick refers to two distinct entities: a play and a racehorse, both of which feature significant elements in their respective contexts.

The play "Dandy Dick" is a comedic work written in three acts by Arthur W. Pinero. It is notable for its historical significance in theater and is recognized for its comedic qualities. The play has been produced by Mr. Daly, particularly featuring Miss Ada Rehan, and has experienced varying levels of success in different locations. The references to the play within the context indicate its influence and the way it engages audiences.

On the other hand, Dandy Dick also refers to a racehorse associated with the characters Sir Tristram and The Dean. Sir Tristram has a financial stake in this horse and shows enthusiasm for its performance in upcoming races, such as the Durnstone Handicap. There is mention of Dandy Dick being a subject of interest for betting opportunities, specifically suggested by the character Georgiana, indicating expectations surrounding its racing capabilities. The horse's condition is frequently discussed, especially its feeding and health status, which is a concern for Georgiana and Sir Tristram due to its involvement in a rescue effort at The Swan Inn.

Interactions around Dandy Dick the horse also reveal its significance beyond mere racing; it appears to be a pivotal point in conversations about winnings, racing strategies, and as a subject of contention among the characters. References suggest that The Dean owns the horse and believes in its potential for victory, while other characters like Blore express concerns about its safety in contexts involving possible harm and poison. 

Thus, Dandy Dick serves as both a cultural artifact in the form of a play and a character in the racing world, each with rich narratives woven into their respective stories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f87fddca66673ee08a52eb11ebbc03dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f4fbe7f763ab4a0c366e0536ed6f49e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bf94783f183c51cfeb7911ef8186ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a623d5c8d4e641231b09b42787ad809b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-797a0e01fc6bbbd28cfe260a566d2a5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9adb1b7231075c5bd3151775f9ce69c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50b102270ef0e98eb298e79412603ac2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5dd7991bf11c9a900752c7c353a0990c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3037df50481adaea6024d7ae1774bd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e596e9ce7637f90613b8207baadae53e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af1eb7dd19180841d20fad29810d6539&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-893320667c086c5f7bc12fc4874ac8b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3b0199cb2086a0bf36a1dde1fbe62d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be2c82e11dcce129d5615a4deb4b0724&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fdfaa450fecffba6a5f866eaa6aeec0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b99d32a6c4077b81dac5436dc1d07de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-852d4b6446a1278be5194702894654c2</data>
