    <node id="Brampton">
      <data key="d0">Brampton</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Brampton is a significant location referenced in various contexts, primarily related to familial interactions and property discussions. It appears as a house and land owned by Mr. Norbury's wife, which the narrator contemplates purchasing, indicating its relevance in property matters.

Historically, Brampton is noted as a place visited by Samuel Pepys's father, who went there to check on Uncle Robert. This connection underscores the role of Brampton in familial relationships and the exchange of information within the family context. The narrator's father resides in Brampton and frequently engages with family communications from this location, as it is the source of papers concerning the uncle's estate that the narrator receives.

Additionally, Brampton serves as a destination for the narrator, who visits it, suggesting its significance as a home or a notable place of residence. The narrative implies that interactions related to estate matters often take place here, further emphasizing Brampton's importance in the family's decisions and history. Thus, Brampton is multifaceted, encompassing themes of home, family, and property.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7930a888490c21dbb03d3e8332e3f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac1ce8602395a115fb7f15acd4fc13c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b994957c96ad60e44faa19f24d657e7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-986db80f0b248cae0ba30adbca747f75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4aff12629bd3d15955833b8aa9864691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-319e15638832916df6b3c2e9cd3f142c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2923fc0c238f18810f053d6e390e261d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80d293af5e7df136bc3f73cb98ae3fad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bcea781bcee54c12362d64f503a3692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e746cb1e34dcaeb58ddb7736aa554abf</data>
