    <node id="John Curgenven">
      <data key="d0">John Curgenven</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">John Curgenven is a multifaceted character from one of the oldest families in Cornwall, recognized for his kind demeanor, which is complemented by a distinctive weathered appearance and a soothing smile. He is closely associated with the local community, establishing connections through his relations with the landlady and other residents. His embodiment of chivalrous behavior is noted as he interacts with locals and visitors alike. 

As a fisherman, John Curgenven played a significant role in maritime rescue operations, notably participating in the rescue of survivors from the shipwreck of the _Brest_. His impressive physical attributes, including a stout arm and an honest face, contribute to his reputation as a capable and trustworthy individual. Additionally, he is recognized as a skilled boatman, possessing extensive knowledge of the sea and local geography, which he shares with those around him. 

John Curgenven is also involved in local commerce, selling serpentine and considered older and less handsome than his wife. His character adds depth to the local narrative, as he provides insights about local landmarks such as the Lizard Lights and recounts his experiences concerning shipwrecks. Furthermore, he is known for offering his company to ladies and engaging in boating activities, often preparing boats for excursions with friends from the narrator's group. This blend of attributes makes John Curgenven a prominent figure embodying both community spirit and maritime heritage in Cornwall.</data>
