    <edge source="Merman" target="Grampus">
      <data key="d7">7.0</data>
      <data key="d8">Merman's realization about the Magicodumbras and Zuzumotzis is prompted by the work of Grampus.&lt;SEP&gt;Merman challenges Grampus's views, believing he possesses a deeper understanding of social origins.&lt;SEP&gt;Merman and Grampus are engaged in a conflict of ideas, with Merman challenging Grampus's interpretations and theories.&lt;SEP&gt;Merman becomes notorious due to the critiques he receives from Grampus, which sparks discussions about his theories.&lt;SEP&gt;Grampus critiques Merman's work, stating that Merman lacks understanding in philology and archaeology.&lt;SEP&gt;Merman perceives Grampus as an adversary whose work undermines his own theory, inciting feelings of jealousy.&lt;SEP&gt;Merman views Grampus's acceptance of ideas that he once proposed as a failure of support from his peers.</data>
      <data key="d9">academic dispute,academic rivalry,adversarial relationship,ambition,challenge,comparison,critique,inspiration,intellectual debate,intellectual discourse,intellectual influence,jealousy,notoriety,response</data>
