    <edge source="Sterne" target="Goethe">
      <data key="d7">9.0</data>
      <data key="d8">The relation between Johann Wolfgang von Goethe and Laurence Sterne highlights a profound literary connection characterized by mutual influence and appreciation. Goethe offers consolation regarding the loss of Sterne, signifying a deep literary bond that transcends temporal boundaries. In multiple reflections, Goethe acknowledges the significant influence Sterne has had on his writings and personal development, underscoring the importance of Sterne’s literary contributions to Goethe's own work.

Goethe examines Sterne's impact not only on his writing but also on his literary status, revealing a nuanced understanding of Sterne's influence within the broader context of German letters. He expresses admiration for Sterne's work, recognizing its role in shaping his narrative style and thematic explorations. This appreciation extends to Goethe's critiques of Sterne’s writing, in which he references Sterne in discussions about various literary styles and narrative techniques.

Furthermore, Goethe acknowledges Sterne's influence on classifications of travelers in his correspondence, indicating a broader impact that goes beyond mere literary appreciation. While Goethe critiques Sterne and his followers through satire, he simultaneously affirms Sterne’s genius, illustrating a complex and layered relationship that balances critique with admiration.

Overall, the connection between Goethe and Sterne showcases the intertwined paths of two literary figures whose works have significantly shaped each other’s contributions to literature.</data>
      <data key="d9">admiration,appreciation,classification,consolation,critique,influence,intertextual relationship,literary appreciation,literary connection,literary criticism,literary influence,literary reference,originality,satire</data>
