    <edge source="Heywood" target="Captain Kneebone">
      <data key="d7">9.0</data>
      <data key="d8">Captain Kneebone and Heywood have a multifaceted relationship characterized by camaraderie and mutual respect. Heywood suggests visiting Captain Kneebone to discuss their plans, indicating their collaborative nature. Their conversations range from discussing illusions and fortunes to the more serious matter of signaling an escape for the Hakka boat from the battlefield, showcasing the breadth of their interactions.

Captain Kneebone is known for his lively demeanor, which surprises Heywood upon his arrival at the nunnery, emphasizing the spirited nature of their friendship. This jovial interaction reflects their close bond, where humor plays a significant role. Their dinners serve as an opportunity for deeper engagement, during which Captain Kneebone shares news of his wealth with Heywood, while Heywood expresses his thoughts on fairness.

The dynamic between them also influences others, as when Captain Kneebone praises Heywood, it affects Rudolph’s feelings towards the captain, suggesting that their relationship is not only relevant to themselves but also impacts their wider circle. Overall, the relationship between Captain Kneebone and Heywood illustrates a blend of friendship, shared adventures, and moments of introspection, all of which highlight the depth and complexity of their connection.</data>
